---------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT THIS PROVES THAT `price_check_stage_one.sql` COULD NOT.

Stage one's probe asserts that the live estate refuses with `no_comparables`.
That is true and it is worth asserting, and on its own it is HALF A TEST. On
an estate where all 64 published listings are examples, a predicate that
correctly excludes examples and a predicate that excludes everything return
the identical answer, which is nothing. A green light of that shape is the
exact blind light that hit this platform twice on 23 September: ledger section
65, where the whole public catalogue was refused to everybody for eleven and a
half hours with every check still green, and section 46.

So this probe puts TWO ROWS in front of `comparable_listings` that differ in
ONE COLUMN and asserts both answers, then flips that one column on a row that
is already in the set and watches it leave and come back. Assertion 3 is the
one that no previous test on this build had: A NON-DEMO LISTING CAN REACH A
COMPARABLE. Assertion 4 is the one that makes 2 and 3 mean something: the row
that left the set is the same row that was in it, so `is_demo` and nothing
else decided.

Assertion 6 is the rule's other half, and it is read AS `anon` because that is
the role a signed-out visitor holds. Example listings are excluded from
statistics and from Price Check comparables ONLY. They are NOT excluded from
search, the home page, the feed or a listing page. A probe that only proved
exclusion would pass just as happily on the day somebody widened the rule and
hid the whole catalogue, which is what section 67 is about.

NOT PROVEN HERE. Nothing on a screen. This container's egress proxy refuses
`CONNECT uccixoonmbhrnyczyigt.supabase.co:443` with 403, so no dev server here
reaches the project and a data-backed route answers the not-found body at HTTP
200. Everything above is the database answering, which is where the predicate
lives.
